  "details": [
   {
    "heading": "Summary",
    "text": "Act early to stop heat loss and add external heat for trauma and burn casualties, upgrading the enclosure system and warming IV/IO fluids."
   },
   {
    "heading": "Key points",
    "items": [
     "Start early, aggressive steps to stop heat loss and add external heat when possible, for trauma and severely burned casualties alike. (p. 8)",
     "Limit exposure to cold ground, wind and air: get insulation under the casualty as soon as possible and keep protective gear on or with them if feasible. (p. 8)",
     "Swap wet clothing for dry if possible. (p. 8)",
     "Put an active heating blanket on the front of the torso and under the arms, never directly on skin or wrapped around the torso, to avoid burns. (p. 8)",
     "Enclose the casualty in the exterior impermeable enclosure bag, then upgrade as soon as possible to a well-insulated system (hooded sleeping bag or other insulation inside the bag). Pre-stage an insulated system with active heating. (p. 8)",
     "Warm IV/IO resuscitation fluids with a battery-powered warmer delivering up to 150 ml/min at a 38°C output. “Use a battery-powered warming device to deliver IV/IO resuscitation fluids, in accordance with current CoTCCC guidelines, at flow rate up to 150 ml/min with a 38°C output temperature.” (p. 8)",
     "Shield the casualty from wind and precipitation on any evacuation platform. (p. 8)"
    ]
   }
  ],
